<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercise xml:id="problem-subbase-of-the-euclidean-topology" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Subbase of the Euclidean topology
  </title>
  
  <statement>
    <p>
      Consider the collections <m> \mathcal{ U } </m> and <m> \mathcal{ D } </m> of upwards- and downwards pointing rays in the real numbers <m> \R </m>, respectively; that is,
      
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \mathcal{ U } \amp = \setmid{ \opint{ a }{ \infty } \in \ps \of{ \R } }{ a \in \R }
        </mrow>
        
        <mrow>
          \mathcal{ D } \amp = \setmid{ \opint{ -\infty }{ b } \in \ps \of{ \R } }{ b \in \R }
        </mrow>
      </md>.
      
      Show that <m> \mathcal{ U } \cup \mathcal{ D } </m> is a subbase of the Euclidean topology <m> \tau_{ d } </m> on <m> \R </m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
  
  <hint>
    <p>
      First argue that every open set in the real line <m> \of{ \R , \tau_{ d } } </m> is a union of bounded open intervals. What do finite intersections of subbasic open sets (elements of <m> \mathcal{ U } \cap \mathcal{ D } </m>) look like?
    </p>
  </hint>
  
  <!-- <solution>
    <p>
      We remark that each set in <m> \mathcal{ U } \cup \mathcal{ D } </m> is open in the Euclidean topology <m> \tau_{ d } </m>; that is, <m> \mathcal{ U } \cup \mathcal{ D } \subseteq \tau_{ d } </m>. Since the topology <m> \gen{ \mathcal{ U } \cup \mathcal{ D } } </m> is the coarsest topology on <m> \R </m> containing <m> \mathcal{ U } \cup \mathcal{ D } </m>, <m> \gen{ \mathcal{ U } \cup \mathcal{ D } } \subseteq \tau_{ d } </m>. It therefore suffices to prove the reverse containment.
    </p>
    
    <p>
      To that end, let <m> U \in \tau_{ d } </m> be an open set in <m> \of{ \R , \tau_{ d } } </m>. Then <m> U </m> is a neighborhood of each of its points, and so for each point <m> u \in U </m>, there is a positive real number <m> r_{ u } \gt 0 </m> so that <m> \mB_{ r_{ u } } \of{ u } \subseteq U </m>. We conclude that
      
      <md>
        U = \bigcup_{ u \in U }{ \mB_{ r_{ u } } \of{ u } } = \bigcup_{ u \in U }{ \of{ \opint{ u - r_{ u } }{ \infty } \cap \opint{ -\infty }{ u + r_{ u } } } }
      </md>.
      
      In particular, <m> U </m> is a union of finite intersections of subbasic open sets in <m> \of{ \R , \gen{ \mathcal{ U } \cup \mathcal{ D } } } </m> and therefore is open in this topology; that is, <m> \tau_{ d } \subseteq \gen{ \mathcal{ U } \cup \mathcal{ D } } </m>.
    </p>
    
    <p>
      Since <m> \gen{ \mathcal{ U } \cup \mathcal{ D } } \subseteq \tau_{ d } </m> and <m> \tau_{ d } \subseteq \gen{ \mathcal{ U } \cup \mathcal{ D } } </m>, we conclude that <m> \gen{ \mathcal{ U } \cup \mathcal{ D } } = \tau_{ d } </m>, and so <m> \mathcal{ U } \cup \mathcal{ D } </m> is a subbase of the Euclidean topology <m> \tau_{ d } </m>.
    </p>
  </solution> -->
</exercise>
